            <div xml:id="d898">

                <pb facs="1088" n="978" xml:id="pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.801 Search/58: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Adams</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-12T13:00:00+08:00">December 12, 1931—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:39 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>125. 1. Yesterday morning about 30 armed and 200 unarmed soldiers belonging to
                    the Chinese salt revenue guard boarded the American motor vessel <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ifung</hi> lying alongside a hulk at Hankow. The
                    American steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Iping</hi> was at the same moment
                    hauling herself alongside the <hi rendition="#italic">Ifung</hi>. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Iping</hi>, downbound from Ichang, had on board an armed
                    naval guard from the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Polos</hi> and also
                    approximately 18 tons of silver reported to belong to the Young Brothers Banking
                    Corporation.</p>
                <p>2. The American armed guard requested the Chinese soldiers to leave the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ifung</hi>. The Chinese soldiers refused to do so. The
                    armed guard thereupon exploded tear gas bombs among the Chinese soldiers who
                    hurriedly left the <hi rendition="#italic">Ifung</hi>. So far as could be
                    ascertained by the American naval authorities no one was injured during the
                    incident.</p>
                <p>3. Shortly thereafter the incident was discussed by the commanding officer of the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Guam</hi> with a Chinese officer who came aboard and
                    who claimed to be the commander of the Fourth Independent Battalion of
                    Government Revenue Guards.</p>
                <p>4. I received my first information regarding the incident yesterday afternoon. I
                    have thus far received no complaint from the Chinese authorities with regard to
                    the incident.</p>
                <p>Legation and Nanking informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Adams</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
